        "id": "fd_0257",
        "title": "Arithmetic Monodromy Determines Spectral Universality Class of Grokking Dynamics",
        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural family of modular learning tasks indexed by a prime p and defined by polynomial maps over F_p, the late-training grokking transition of gradient flow on sufficiently wide networks falls into finitely many spectral universality classes, and the class is determined by the geometric monodromy group of the task map (e.g. full, solvable, or imprimitive monodromy). Precisely, after rescaling time by a task-dependent power of p, the Hessian eigenvalue trajectory near grokking converges to one of a small number of limiting profiles depending only on the monodromy type, not on architecture details. Test: Construct task families with provably different monodromy groups but matched input/output size and degree; train multiple architectures across primes p; measure Hessian spectra and grokking-time rescaled dynamics; confirm if curves collapse within monodromy class and separate across classes. Refutation occurs if no architecture-robust spectral collapse appears or if monodromy type fails to predict the limiting profile. Impact: This would connect algebraic geometry and representation theory to learning dynamics, yielding a structural predictor of when and how grokking occurs and suggesting principled task design for controllable phase transitions in neural systems.",
