        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a natural infinite family of formally checkable integer-sequence theorems {T_n} defined by polynomial recurrences and divisibility constraints such that the minimal proof length of T_n in a fixed arithmetic proof system undergoes sharp phase transitions exactly at indices n where the Newton polygon of the associated p-adic recurrence operator changes combinatorial type for some prime p. Test: Construct several recurrence families (e.g. Somos-type, Ap\u00e9ry-type, or linear recurrences with polynomial coefficients), compute the p-adic Newton polygon stratification of the recurrence operator as n varies, and compare these predicted transition points against empirical minimal proof lengths or search complexity in Lean/SAT/SMT encodings. The conjecture is supported if proof complexity spikes align with polygon-type changes significantly better than with size, degree, or coefficient height baselines; it is refuted if no such alignment appears across natural families. Impact: This would introduce a genuinely arithmetic-geometric order parameter for proof difficulty, linking p-adic analysis, symbolic recurrence theory, and automated reasoning, and could enable complexity forecasting and curriculum design for theorem provers on number-theoretic statements.",
