        "id": "fd_0354",
        "title": "Proof-Theoretic Phase Transitions in Random Simplicial Complexes",
        "description": "Conjecture: For Linial\u2013Meshulam random 2-complexes Y(n,p), there exists a sharp threshold p = n^(-1/2+o(1)) at which the minimal resolution proof width of unsatisfiable cohomological encoding formulas for H^1(Y; F_2)=0 drops from polynomially large to O(log n) with high probability. Test: Generate the standard CNF encodings of nontrivial 1-cocycles/non-coboundaries on Y(n,p), measure empirical minimal proof width/space across p, and compare against topological thresholds for vanishing first cohomology; confirmation is a width collapse aligned with the topology threshold, refutation is persistent proof hardness away from or misaligned with that threshold. Impact: Establishes a direct quantitative bridge between random topology and proof complexity, suggesting that topological phase transitions govern automated reasoning difficulty and enabling topology-aware SAT benchmark design.",
