        "title": "Prime-Spectral Universality for Higher-Dimensional Expanders",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists an explicit infinite family of bounded-degree 2-dimensional simplicial expanders X_n such that for every sufficiently large prime p, the normalized spectrum of the up-Laplacian on 1-cochains with coefficients in F_p converges to a limit law \u03bc_p, and the family {\u03bc_p}_p is non-universal: specifically, there exist infinitely many primes p,q with total variation distance d_TV(\u03bc_p, \u03bc_q) > c for some absolute constant c > 0. Test: Construct candidate Ramanujan-type complexes or coset complexes, compute mod-p up-Laplacian spectra for growing n and varying p, and check whether the empirical spectral measures stabilize in n but remain separated across infinitely many primes. Refutation occurs if the limiting law is eventually independent of p or the cross-prime distances vanish. Impact: This would reveal a genuinely arithmetic obstruction to spectral universality in high-dimensional expansion, linking expander theory, modular representation phenomena, and topological data analysis over finite fields.",
