        "id": "fd_0370",
        "title": "Homological Memory of Proof Search Trajectories",
        "description": "Conjecture: For any sufficiently large formal theorem library equipped with a fixed tactic language, there exists a canonical filtered simplicial complex built from proof states encountered during successful and failed searches such that the persistence landscape of its first homology predicts cross-theorem transferability of lemmas: specifically, theorem families whose search complexes have matching H1 barcodes up to bottleneck distance \u03b5 admit lemma-transfer policies achieving a statistically significant reduction in median proof length compared to barcode-dissimilar families. Test: Construct proof-state complexes from multiple benchmark libraries, compute persistent homology, cluster theorem families by barcode similarity, and evaluate whether transferring discovered lemmas/tactics within clusters beats matched out-of-cluster controls under preregistered metrics. Refutation occurs if barcode similarity has no predictive power after controlling for theorem size/domain. Impact: This would turn topological signatures of search into a new invariant for automated theorem proving, enabling geometry-aware curriculum design, lemma mining, and transfer learning across distant mathematical domains.",
