        "description": "Conjecture: For any sufficiently large corpus of formally verified theorems over arithmetic or algebraic objects defined over number fields, there exists a canonical construction of a proof-dependency simplicial complex K together with arithmetic labels on simplices such that the prime-indexed torsion persistence profile of K is asymptotically non-uniform and correlates with splitting statistics of the underlying field extensions; specifically, for families of theorem corpora parametrized by extensions L/K, the p-torsion barcode density differs on a set of primes of positive Dirichlet density according to the Frobenius conjugacy class distribution in Gal(L/K). Test: Build labeled dependency complexes from multiple formal corpora involving objects over distinct number fields, compute prime-wise torsion persistence, and compare the resulting prime distributions against Chebotarev-predicted splitting classes; confirmation requires statistically significant agreement beyond null models based only on graph size/degree distributions, while failure of any positive-density separation refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would create a new bridge between arithmetic geometry, topological data analysis, and formalized mathematics, suggesting that deep Galois-theoretic structure leaves measurable topological fingerprints in proof organization and enabling arithmetic-aware theorem retrieval and corpus classification.",
