        "id": "fd_0365",
        "title": "Spectral Universality of Proof-State Graphs Across Formal Systems",
        "description": "Conjecture: For large theorem corpora formalized in distinct proof assistants or logical foundations (e.g. Lean, Coq, Isabelle, first-order ATP traces), if one builds proof-state transition graphs using a normalized notion of local inference state, then after degree normalization their Laplacian spectral measures converge to the same nontrivial limiting family up to corpus-size scaling; moreover, deviations from this universal spectrum predict domain-specific proof difficulty and transfer-learning failure. Test: Construct proof-state graphs from multiple libraries and automated proof traces, compute normalized spectral densities and finite-size scaling exponents, and statistically test whether a shared limiting law fits all systems better than system-specific unrelated models; refute by exhibiting persistent spectral incompatibility after normalization. Impact: This would suggest that formal reasoning has foundation-independent mesoscopic geometry, enabling universal proof-search heuristics, cross-system benchmark transfer, and a new quantitative theory of mathematical proof complexity.",
