        "description": "Conjecture: For every finite simplicial complex K and homological degree i, there exists a finite set of primes S_i(K) and a rational constant lambda_i(K) >= 1 such that along iterated barycentric subdivisions sd^m(K), the p-primary torsion in H_i(sd^m(K); Z) is eventually trivial for all p notin S_i(K), while for each p in S_i(K) the quantity log_p |Tor_p H_i(sd^m(K); Z)| either stays bounded or grows linearly in m with slope exactly lambda_{i,p}(K). Test: Compute integral homology of sd^m(K) for many complexes and m, prime-decompose torsion, and check whether only finitely many primes persist and whether p-adic torsion sizes fit an eventual linear-in-m law; a single family exhibiting infinitely many new persistent primes or superlinear/sublinear unbounded growth refutes it. Impact: This would reveal a rigid arithmetic dynamical law for how combinatorial refinement creates or suppresses torsion, linking topological subdivision, p-adic asymptotics, and algorithmic prediction of homological complexity.",
