        "id": "fd_0382",
        "title": "Proof-Complexity Holography via Zeta Spectra of Resolution Configuration Graphs",
        "description": "Conjecture: For every unsatisfiable CNF family {F_n}, define the directed configuration graph G(F_n) whose vertices are clause configurations reachable under a fixed complete resolution strategy, and let Z_F(u) be the Ihara-style zeta function of the underlying non-backtracking configuration graph. There exists a universal constant c > 0 such that if the second pole radius of Z_F(u) is at most 1/c times the inverse branching factor, then every resolution refutation of F requires clause space at least c times the logarithm of the number of distinct simple prime cycles in G(F). Test: Construct configuration graphs for benchmark CNF families (pebbling, pigeonhole, Tseitin, random k-SAT near threshold), compute/approximate their non-backtracking spectra and prime-cycle counts, and compare against known or experimentally measured clause-space lower bounds. Refutation occurs if families with small second pole radius and many prime cycles admit low-space refutations, or if high-space families systematically fail the predicted spectral signature. Impact: This would create a new spectral-dynamical invariant for proof complexity, linking zeta functions, cycle growth, and memory lower bounds, and could yield automated hardness predictors for SAT and theorem proving.",
