        "id": "fd_0383",
        "title": "Persistent Cohomology Predicts Proof Search Dead-Ends",
        "description": "Conjecture: For theorem-proving state graphs generated by a fixed prover and tactic set on a benchmark family, the presence of a long-lived 1-dimensional persistent cohomology class in the local search-state complex around a goal implies an exponentially larger expected time-to-solution than for matched goals with trivial H1 persistence, after controlling for graph size and degree statistics. Test: Build search-state graphs from many proof attempts, compute persistent cohomology of neighborhoods or filtration by tactic-cost/confidence, and statistically test whether long persistence in H1 predicts provable runtime blowup versus baselines using only standard graph features. Refutation occurs if H1 persistence adds no predictive power or fails to correlate with hardness on held-out theorem families. Impact: This would introduce a topological obstruction theory for automated reasoning, yielding new hardness diagnostics, better proof-guidance features, and a principled way to redesign tactic languages to avoid topological traps.",
