        "id": "fd_0384",
        "title": "Renormalization Fixed Point for Resolution Proof Search",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a coarse-graining operator R on resolution configuration graphs such that for random k-CNF families in the critical window, the sequence R^m(G(F_n)) converges (after appropriate rescaling of size and edge weights) to one of finitely many universality classes, and the limiting class determines asymptotic proof complexity exponents for width, space, and length. Test: Define an explicit coarse-graining on configuration graphs, compute the induced flow on large ensembles of random and structured CNFs, and check whether instances with the same limiting fixed point share the same asymptotic scaling laws while different fixed points yield statistically distinct exponents. Refutation occurs if no stable coarse-grained fixed points emerge or if the fixed point class fails to predict measured proof-complexity scaling. Impact: This would import renormalization-group ideas into proof complexity, potentially giving a new macroscopic theory of SAT hardness and a principled way to classify solver difficulty beyond clause-variable ratio heuristics.",
