        "title": "Homological Phase Transition in Proof Search State Complexes",
        "description": "Conjecture: For a fixed theorem domain and prover, build the simplicial complex whose vertices are reachable proof states within depth d and whose simplices are mutually one-step compatible tactic extensions. There exists a critical scaling regime d = c log n (with n the statement size) where hard statements are characterized by persistent first-homology intervals of length polynomially larger than those of easy statements, while easy statements have only short-lived cycles. Test: Generate these state complexes for benchmark families in Lean/Coq, compute persistent homology across depth and compatibility thresholds, and check whether long H1 bars predict proof length/search failure significantly better than standard graph features (branching factor, node count, heuristic score variance). Refutation occurs if no statistically stable separation appears across domains or if homology adds no predictive power beyond existing search metrics. Impact: This would turn proof hardness into a measurable topological order parameter, enabling topology-aware proof guidance, adaptive search restarts, and a new bridge between automated reasoning, TDA, and complexity theory.",
