        "id": "fd_0388",
        "title": "Profinite Stability Fingerprint for Polynomial Dynamics",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let f(x) in Z[x] be a polynomial of degree >= 2 with nonzero discriminant. Define its finite-dynamics fingerprint at modulus n to be the tuple of cycle-count statistics of the map x -> f(x) mod n across all prime powers dividing n. Then, outside a zero-density set of primes, two polynomials f,g in Z[x] are affine-conjugate over Q if and only if their fingerprints agree for all sufficiently large prime powers p^k up to some uniform bounded statistic depth B(deg f, deg g). Test: Compute fingerprints for large families of non-conjugate and conjugate polynomials over many primes and prime powers; confirmation is separation of all non-conjugate pairs by bounded-depth local dynamical data, while a counterexample is a non-conjugate pair with matching fingerprints on almost all p^k at all tested depths. Impact: This would create a new local-to-global rigidity principle for arithmetic dynamics, giving a finite-data method to classify polynomial dynamical systems and linking p-adic dynamics, finite field dynamics, and computational invariant theory.",
