        "id": "fd_0389",
        "title": "Spectral Universality of Proof-State Complexes",
        "description": "Conjecture: For any finitely presented proof system with local inference rules (e.g. resolution, term rewriting, or typed lambda reduction), form the simplicial complex whose vertices are proof states reachable within k steps from an initial instance and whose simplices are pairwise jointly reachable compatible states. After degree normalization, the first nonzero Laplacian eigenvalue of this complex exhibits a universality law: across random instances in the same hardness class, it concentrates around a system-dependent constant, and sharp drops in this eigenvalue occur if and only if there exist short certificates/refutations. Test: Explicitly generate proof-state complexes for families of SAT instances, rewrite systems, and lambda terms at increasing k; compute normalized Hodge/Laplacian spectra; check whether eigenvalue concentration holds within hardness classes and whether low spectral gap predicts short proofs better than existing graph features. Refutation occurs if spectra fail to concentrate or if gap drops do not correlate with certificate existence. Impact: This would introduce a cross-domain spectral order parameter for proof complexity, enabling hardness prediction, adaptive theorem proving, and a new bridge between proof theory, topological data analysis, and random complex theory.",
