        "description": "Conjecture: For proof-search state graphs arising from a fixed theorem domain and tactic system, the average Ollivier-Ricci curvature of the reachable subgraph at resource bound R has a sharp positive correlation with eventual solve probability and expected proof length, uniformly across theorem families after normalization for graph size and degree. In particular, there exists a domain-dependent threshold kappa_c such that instances whose explored proof-state graph has normalized average curvature above kappa_c are solvable with polynomially bounded expected search time, while those below kappa_c exhibit superpolynomial search growth. Test: Generate proof-state graphs from Lean/ATP searches across many theorem families, compute discrete Ricci curvature statistics on partial search frontiers, and evaluate whether curvature thresholding predicts success/failure and runtime better than baseline graph features (degree, clustering, expansion, cycle counts, Betti numbers). Refutation occurs if curvature adds no predictive power or no threshold behavior appears. Impact: This would introduce a geometric invariant for proof complexity, enabling curvature-aware proof guidance, early hardness detection, and a new bridge between optimal transport, discrete geometry, and automated reasoning.",
