        "id": "fd_0637",
        "title": "Direction 4: Integration with Practical E-Graph Systems",
        "description": "**Conjecture:** The semantic soundness framework can be instantiated with the concrete e-graph implementation of the egg library, yielding a verified higher-order optimizer that matches or beats GHC's simplifier on a benchmark of 100 Haskell Core expressions.\n\n**Test:** Implement the `HOEGraph` interface over egg's Rust e-graph. Translate 100 GHC Core expressions into `HOTerm` syntax. Run bounded saturation with \u03b2/\u03b7/case-of-case rules. Compare:\n1. Output term size vs. GHC -O2 output size.\n2. Execution time of optimized programs.\n3. Semantic equivalence on test inputs.\n\n**Impact:** Direct practical impact on functional language compilation. A verified optimizer that matches GHC's simplifier would be the first formally guaranteed compiler optimization pass for a major functional language.\n\n**Catalog References:** `Catalog/Pythagorean/HigherOrderEqSat.lean` (ho_extraction_semantics_preserved, hoEqvGen_egraph_sound).\n\n**Proof Strategy:** The key is instantiating `HOEGraph.sameClass` with egg's union-find class relation and proving that each egg merge operation preserves `HOEGraphSound`. This requires showing that egg's congruence closure correctly handles the binder-aware congruence rules.\n\n**Domain Bridges:** Compiler engineering, systems programming, performance optimization.\n\n**Lineage:** Applies `ho_extraction_semantics_preserved` to a concrete implementation.\n\n**Ambition:** Medium. The formal side is a straightforward instantiation; the engineering side requires significant implementation work.\n\n---",
